# An entry goes missing on some node while the network's membership churns: 19
# nodes join and leave nw0 throughout, entries are created among the churn, and at
# the end one node is short of one of them.
#
# Which entry is lost is not fixed. Over four runs this scenario lost vfaoqbzt
# three times and bakk once, so whatever goes wrong is not tied to a particular
# entry -- which also means no variant of this scenario with an entry removed
# reliably passes, and its cost cannot be measured that way. Use
# -networkdb.convergence-measure, which times only the executions which converged.
#
# Fixed by moby/moby#53437, which made bulk syncs carry network attachments. This
# failed one execution in eight at worst before that landed, and has since run 7,104
# consecutive executions without failing. The rates recorded below are from before
# the fix; they are what the attempts are sized against, so that a regression is
# caught rather than merely likely to be.
#
# moby/moby#53142 left every one of these rates untouched, so the credit is
# #53437's rather than the run of NetworkDB fixes in general.
#
# Found by TestNetworkDBAlwaysConverges on this tree, at maxDelay=3, so its actions
# land 1-3ms apart: tight clumping rather than either extreme. Kept unreduced.
# Reducing it is possible and a bad trade: -networkdb.convergence-minimize=optimize
# walked it down to 13 nodes and 8 actions and reported that form as 1 in 404,
# against single digits here, and chose to keep this one.
#
# Measured before the fix, four times with -networkdb.convergence-measure, 12
# trials apiece, at about 1 in 4, 1 in 6, 1 in 7 and 1 in 8, and 310ms an
# execution. The 37 attempts below are sized from the worst of those rather than
# the average: a budget is only worth stating if it holds on a bad day, and these
# four estimates spread by a factor of two. At 1 in 8 they give about a 99% chance
# of catching it, for about 11.5s on a run which does not fail. That cost is from
# before the fix too; measured on the fixed tree this scenario runs in about
# 10.3s.
#
# Sizing from the first measurement alone put this at 19, which is 99% against 1 in
# 4 and only 92% against 1 in 8 -- one run in twelve passing with the bug present.
#
# Cost did not pick between the three scenarios here. Once each was measured
# repeatedly and sized from its own worst rate they landed within about 15% of one
# another -- 11.5s here against 9.8s and 10.4s -- where sizing from single
# measurements had made this one look half the price of the others. Those figures
# are priced at pre-fix per-execution costs; measured on the fixed tree the three
# run in about 10.3s, 2.8s and 6.9s, so this one is now the dearest by some way.
# On readability it is the worst of the three, so prefer
# entry-after-leave-rejoin.scenario both for understanding the bug and as the
# regression test, and keep this one for the membership churn it covers and they
# do not.
scenario nodes=19 seed=0x3e664 attempts=37
    +2ms #17:JoinNetwork(nw0)
    +3ms #17:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
    +2ms #3:JoinNetwork(nw0)
         #3:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
    +2ms #0:JoinNetwork(nw0)
    +3ms #0:CreateEntry(nw0, vaz=bnwdc)
    +3ms #0:LeaveNetwork(nw0)
